.TH tupath n 0.1 tclutils
.SH tupath
.PP
Predictable path helpers around Tcl's \fBfile\fR command, plus a purely lexical \fBclean\fR (works on non\-existent paths) and \fBcommonPath\fR.
.SS Commands
.TS
l l.
Command	Result
\fBnormalize path\fR	absolute path, resolves \fB.\fR/\fB..\fR and symlinks (\fBfile normalize\fR)
\fBclean path\fR	lexical cleanup of \fB.\fR/\fB..\fR/\fB//\fR \fBwithout\fR touching the filesystem
\fBisAbsolute path\fR	1/0
\fBcomponents path\fR	list of path parts (\fBfile split\fR)
\fBjoin args...\fR	join parts (\fBfile join\fR)
\fBrelative base target\fR	lexical path of \fBtarget\fR relative to \fBbase\fR
\fBcommonPath {paths}\fR	longest shared directory prefix, or \fB""\fR if none
\fBreadlink path\fR	symlink target (\fB{TCLUTILS TUPATH NOTLINK}\fR if not a link)
.TE
.CS
package require tclutils::tupath
tupath::clean      a/./b/../c           ;# a/c
tupath::commonPath {/a/b/c /a/b/d}      ;# /a/b
tupath::relative   /a/b /a/b/c/d        ;# c/d
.CE
.PP
\fBclean\fR and \fBrelative\fR are purely lexical, so they are safe for paths that do not exist; \fBnormalize\fR and \fBreadlink\fR consult the filesystem.
